																		The Bone Seer
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sir-piecemaker" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7100466); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7100466&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608990577" alt="Sir Piecemaker" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7100466)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sir-piecemaker" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7100466); return false;" >Sir Piecemaker</a></span> <span class="odate time_1608223535 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">17 Dec 2020 16:45</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4847100">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Mystery</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> Standard SCP document with a large focus on interview/experiment logs</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> A woman capable of gaining information about others by handling their bones (as well as passing the information on) turns herself in to the Foundation, motivated by a steadfast belief that a K-class scenario is coming.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> After an unrelated incident in which an agent was KIA, the SCP, then working as a mortician, becomes aware of the Foundation via handling the agent's bones and shortly after turns itself in to the Foundation. After the Foundation ensures it's not a GOI agent, the SCP is taken in.</p>
<p>The SCP is able to gain information through physical contact with the bones of others (by what it claims is &quot;listening to the Song&quot;) and carve the bones with various occult symbols to allow others to similarly glean the information. It claims its abilities are not born/naturally occurring but rather were taught to it by its grandmother. As the SCP has no living family, the info can not be verified. No attempts to recreate the scrimshaw by using the same symbols or teach others to &quot;listen to the Song&quot; have been successful.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The SCP is motivated by a zealous belief that a K-class scenario is coming soon, though it doesn't know when or how, and the details it describes do not match any known K-class scenarios. It's unclear whether the claim is valid or merely religious - possibly a side effect of the repeated use of its abilities, as it can sometimes have problems distinguishing its own memories from the ones gained via its abilities. This belief does however leave it completely cold and apathetic to individual lives, including its own, as well as highly cooperative with the Foundation, believing it will be necessary to try and piece the world together after the cataclysm.</p>
<p>The Foundation is left trying to find out how reliable is the SCP itself and its claims, as well the extent of its abilities to gain and pass information and whether it could be useful, though it remains wary of possible memetic agents.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The reader is left trying to figure out exactly how the SCP works and thinks, how sane it is, and can then turn to the experiment logs to see how various test subjects deal with having the memories of others, sometimes not even human.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I can provide additional details in comments, but I'm currently at the exact word limit.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
