			<div class="content" id="post-content-4853873">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The SCP is motivated by a zealous belief that a K-class scenario is coming soon, though it doesn't know when or how, and the details it describes do not match any known K-class scenarios.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I really like this idea of reading the bones but I am unconvinced about the entire thing centered around a vague K-class event. That's no way to build stakes.</p>
<p>I think this could work without the vague threat, make it something a bit more grounded&#8230; like the Seer is worried about being hunted by something or someone (but don't be vague, be mysterious), while you sell it with how upset/scared they are and are looking for security in containment. (<em>this is just an idea, you don't have to take the suggestion of course</em>)</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>This belief does however leave it completely cold and apathetic to individual lives, including its own, as well as highly cooperative with the Foundation, believing it will be necessary to try and piece the world together after the cataclysm.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>also not convinced that being sure the world would end would then lead to these results&#8230; like maybe the cooperation, but why would it make the SCP apathetic about the individual lives? That's fine if you wanna make them cold towards individual lives, but it doesn't make much sense this would be the reason personally.</em></p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>can then turn to the experiment logs to see how various test subjects deal with having the memories of others</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Also not really sold on this idea, I don't think experiment logs is the way to go here. Instead you could summarize what research was done with the results and then unpack them in conversations or observation reports from the researchers.</em></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Overall</span></strong>, I like the idea of the core anomaly&#8230; but I think some unpacking of the actual anomaly would be more interesting than the focus on some vague end of the world. Most of the compelling SCPs I have read have not had such HUGE stakes. I think this would work better with a more grounded relatable story about a person who can see into weird shit and how its affected them&#8230; Maybe some of the history of the practice, where this person has been/where they've used it before, and some unpacking of their psychological status as a result.</p>

							</div>

								
					
